-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 0060 — FORTY HARBOURS STOP SAILING OVERLAND
--        Every port that had no water of its own gets the water it actually worked, and stops
--        being handed a land-exempt corridor across the country it stands in.
-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
--
-- GENERATED by scripts/build-sea-migration.mjs — do not hand-edit. Change the sea (scripts/
-- sea-grid.mjs) or the ports and cut a NEW migration; an applied one is history.
--
-- ── THE OWNER, VERBATIM (docs/OWNER_REQUESTS.md row 41) ────────────────────────────────────────
--   "i don't want the fleet to ever touch land."
--
-- ── WHAT THIS SUPERSEDES ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
--   It SUPERSEDES THE DATA of 0052 (the Severn is water), which itself superseded 0046's:
--   public.sea_raster's one row is rewritten and public.sea_reaches is replaced wholesale. 0046
--   and 0052 stay exactly as they applied — they are history, and editing one is the D23 defect.
--   It supersedes NO FUNCTION and re-cuts none. voyage.path_nm and voyage.path_refusal are
--   0046's and are untouched; voyage.sail_refusal belongs to 0050. A generator that re-emitted
--   the bodies it remembered would silently roll 0050 back, so this file emits DATA only.
--   It also PATCHES 55 row(s) of 0040's membership raster (public.sea_cells): the cells these
--   channels open were land when 0040 was cut, and land carries no sea name. Each one joins the
--   nearest named sea BY WATER — build-sea-raster.mjs's own rule for unnamed water, reused, not
--   re-invented — and each is asserted by name below.
--
-- ── THE DEFECT, WHICH IS 0052'S DEFECT AT THIRTY-NINE MORE HARBOURS ────────────────────────────
--   public.sea_reaches.snap_nm is not a label. voyage.path_refusal takes it as the LAND-EXEMPT
--   HEAD ALLOWANCE of a course, so a harbour whose own 0.25° cell scan-filled as land is handed
--   a straight line over the country it stands in — and the pathfinder uses it. 0052 proved
--   that at Bristol (64.55 nm across Devon) and fixed her; MEASURED on the raster this file
--   supersedes, 40 places still snapped more than 20 nm, 13 of them more than 30 nm:
--     LNG  Longyearbyen    67.68 nm  →   0.00 nm
--     HAN  Hanoi           58.68 nm  →   0.00 nm
--     KHA  Khambhat        57.77 nm  →   0.00 nm
--     TOK  Tokyo           47.69 nm  →   9.83 nm
--     PAT  Patras          47.21 nm  →   0.00 nm
--     TRO  Trondheim       45.98 nm  →   0.00 nm
--     IZM  Izmir           40.29 nm  →   0.00 nm
--     AMS  Amsterdam       35.47 nm  →   8.99 nm
--     CAL  Calabar         34.68 nm  →   0.00 nm
--     FUZ  Fuzhou          32.77 nm  →   0.00 nm
--     HRN  Hoorn           32.76 nm  →   0.00 nm
--     JAM  Jamestown       31.75 nm  →   0.00 nm
--     THE  Thessaloniki    31.06 nm  →   0.00 nm
--     … and 27 more between 20 and 30 nm, every one listed in the guard below.
--   Longyearbyen's 67.68 nm ran clean across Spitsbergen; Hanoi's 58.68 nm across the Tonkin
--   delta; Tokyo's 47.69 nm across the Boso peninsula; Lisbon — the capital of the Portuguese
--   seaborne empire — snapped 23.30 nm over Estremadura because the Tagus is narrower than a
--   cell. Fuzhou snapped to the WRONG SEA as well as the wrong side: the East China Sea, while
--   the port declares the Taiwan Strait.
--
-- ── THE FIX: 62 CHANNELS ENTRIES, EACH A WATER A SHIP OF THE PERIOD WORKED ──────────────────
--   scripts/sea-grid.mjs holds the one list of "water the map is too coarse to draw". 38 of its
--   62 entries open water this raster did not have — new ones, plus two extended in place (the
--   Gulf of Khambhat to its head at Cambay; the White Sea road up the Northern Dvina to
--   Arkhangelsk). Every entry is justified from the
--   period, in the file, beside its points — the Marsdiep and Texel Roads where the VOC fleets
--   made up; the Uraga Channel and Edo Bay, which carried the whole coastal trade of Japan; the
--   Gulf of Patras, where Lepanto was fought by four hundred galleys; the Cross River, which
--   Bristol and Liverpool ships worked fifty miles to Duke Town; Isfjorden, whaled from the
--   1610s. Three are ROADSTEADS rather than straits — Valencia, Safi, the Douro bar — where the
--   110 m coastline simply bulges seaward of the real shore and swallowed the open sea a
--   harbour lay in. Two are named in the file as the WEAKEST justifications here and open the
--   estuary only, not the river: the Taedong (Nampo) and Gyeonggi Bay (Incheon), whose towns
--   are 19th-century treaty ports even though their approaches are older.
--
-- ── WHAT MOVED, MEASURED, AND WHAT DID NOT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
--   water cells        545,989  →  546,095   (+106 opened, -0 closed)
--   snap readings      40 moved, and all 40 are harbours that were over 20 nm. No harbour
--                      already inside the threshold moved at all.
--   pair readings      of 56,406 directed readings, 43,632 are IDENTICAL and 12,774 moved.
--                      12,718 have an end in the 40 fixed harbours — that is the fix, and it
--                      moves distances UP: these harbours were starting their voyages in
--                      somebody else's sea. Amsterdam now leaves by the Marsdiep instead of
--                      stepping onto the North Sea across North Holland.
--                      56 are between two harbours this file gave no new water to, and none
--                      by more than 12.4 nm (BRU→HAM 339.1→351.5) or 3.66%. Two
--                      causes, both geometry rather than error: a cell opened for one harbour
--                      can be a cell a PASSING route now clips, and the figure is the length of
--                      the greedy-straightened polyline, so a different cell path can straighten
--                      a little longer. 2 grew, 54 shrank; the cap on the SHRINKING side is
--                      the one that matters, because a shortcut is always a shortening.
--   THIS IS WHERE A CHANNEL BECOMES A CANAL, and the cap caught one during the build: carried
--   the last twelve miles up the Trave to Lübeck's own quay, the fix put sailable water 22 nm
--   from Hamburg's, inside the 27.5 nm approach the straightener exempts — and Hamburg→Tallinn
--   fell from 991.5 nm round Denmark to 611.0 nm STRAIGHT OVER SCHLESWIG. A Kiel Canal, 1895.
--   The channel now stops at Travemünde, where the seagoing water stopped.
--   THE RASTER ONLY OPENS WATER (0 cells closed, asserted at generation). That is a production
--   rule, not a tidiness one: a voyage already at sea carries a frozen path computed over the
--   OLD raster, and a course legal over a raster stays legal over a superset of it. Closing a
--   cell could strand a fleet mid-passage on water that had become land.
--   UNMOVED, asserted below to 0.1 nm — none of these ports is one of the 40:
--     the Cape, no Suez                 ALE→ADE 11049.5 nm
--     the Horn, no Panama               VER→ACA 12397.8 nm
--     the Horn from the east            BUE→CLL 4251.9 nm
--     the Roaring Forties               RIO→CEB 9801.1 nm
--     the Cape of Good Hope             CPT→JAK 5244.9 nm
--     Valparaiso to the Cape            VLP→CPT 5210.3 nm
--   MOVED ON PURPOSE, because Lisbon is one of the 40: LIS→NAG 12991.5 nm (was
--   12989.3), still far above the 12,000 nm floor that says the Arctic has not reopened.
--
-- ── WHAT THIS FILE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO, AND WHAT IT FOUND WHILE NOT DOING IT ────────────
--   1. IT DOES NOT CLOSE THE SIX CHANNELS ENTRIES WHOSE OWN INTERPOLATION DRAWS A CANAL.
--      Measured 2026-08-26: an entry's points are joined cell by cell, so an entry holding TWO
--      unconnected waters opens the land between them. `irrawaddy-sittaung` joins Yangon to the
--      Chao Phraya across 330 nm and opens 30 land cells, one 85.6 nm up in the Tenasserim
--      mountains — the Gulf of Thailand reaches the Andaman Sea in 382 nm instead of the
--      ~2,300 nm round Singapore. `elbe-weser`, `thames-scheldt`, `gironde`, `baltic-gulfs` and
--      `gambia-senegal` spill the same way, 31-63 nm inland. CLOSING water can strand a voyage
--      already sailing it; that needs a migration which deals with those fleets first.
--   2. IT DOES NOT REPAIR THE COURSE PROPOSER, AND THE PROPOSER IS WORSE THAN ANY SNAP.
--      The client proposes a course and the server verifies it. The proposer asks "is this leg
--      water?" by SAMPLING every half cell (src/lib/sea/pathfind.ts:segmentIsWater), which is
--      phase-dependent and skips land that falls inside an approach allowance. Measured on the
--      raster this file supersedes:
--        * PANAMA CITY→PORT ROYAL IS SERVED AT 560.9 nm. The Isthmus of Panama is about
--          30 nm wide at 0.25° and Panama's approach allowance is 32.0 nm (snap 10.82 + one cell
--          diagonal), so every land sample on the straight line falls inside it and the whole
--          10,479.8 nm road round the Horn collapses into one leg ACROSS THE ISTHMUS. A Panama
--          Canal, 1914, in the served table today. Panama→Santiago de Cuba likewise: 1,944.6 nm
--          for a road of 11,023.4. Neither canal control in this chain could see it, because
--          Veracruz and Acapulco do not lie either side of the same 30 nm neck. THIS FILE DOES
--          NOT CHANGE IT: PAN→POR reads 560.9 nm after this file, exactly as before.
--        * The same weakness makes the proposer disagree with the server's own land guard: a
--          stored course is re-split at every sea boundary (voyage.segments_from_course, 0047)
--          and re-sampled in a different phase, so a leg accepted as water can be refused as
--          land. On this raster Lisbon→Nagasaki is one: legal as proposed, E_LAND at
--          (23.23°, -16.48°) once stored. scripts/db/proofs/09_the_fleet_never_touches_land.sql
--          IS RED FOR THAT REASON with this file applied, and it is right to be — the course
--          does touch land. The repair is a sound proposer (walk the cells a leg crosses, and
--          forbid A* the corner cuts a fixed-step sampler cannot judge), which re-prices every
--          road in the world and needs its own migration and its own balance pass. It was built
--          and measured in this worktree and deliberately NOT shipped here: it moved 50,868 of
--          56,406 readings and disconnected 10 ports whose channels are only diagonally linked.
--   3. It does not touch the mover, the ports, the goods or any price, and re-cuts no function.
--      It does not change the 20 nm threshold or the +25 nm cell the mover adds to a snap.
--
-- Depends on: 0002 (ports, seas, gc_distance_nm), 0040 (sea_cells, voyage.sea_at), 0046 (the two
-- tables and voyage.path_nm / voyage.path_refusal), 0052 (the data this supersedes), and the
-- port rows of 0003/0036/0041.
-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

-- ── The raster, superseding 0052's ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Same shape, same LSB-first packing, same two bits per cell (bit 0 SEA, bit 1 POLAR). An
-- UPDATE, not an insert: 0046's row is the one row, and this file rewrites what is in it.
update public.sea_raster
   set cols = 1440, rows = 720, cell_deg = 0.25, bits_per_cell = 2,
       cells = decode('', 'base64')
 where id = 1;

